From my understanding, one of the principles of effective flow is to use
pull-based queues as much as possible.

I'm wondering how this applies in terms of AI-assisted programming. For
example, I usually think of working with a coding agent as assigning it
a task - pushing a message to the agent.

However I wonder if it's more helpful to think of it as a pull model. I
push something onto the queue (so does this end up being push, pull, or
both)? Then an agent takes it off the queue.

Where I'm particularly interested in this is in developing more
autonomous agents. So rather than instructing an agent to code review -
I would rather post to the list, let the various agents monitor the
list, and respond to messages if it's appropriate. For example, a code
review agent doesn't need to respond to an RFC - unless perhaps the RFC
is about code review specifically, in which case it might have something
to contribute.

The point is, rather than thinking of agents as buttons to push when I
want a certain task done - they become more ambient and proactive,
detecting when they can provide value and contributing to the
discussion. In that sense they work like real collaborators, not simply
workers that I delegate to.

I'd like some feedback on the high-level principles of flow, how they
apply to working with coding agents, and what are current lines of
thinking around this.
